ements are true?\n\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n- The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. From the diagram below we see that $F$ acts perpendicular to $n$ at every point on $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/844d5e5574571ae221a86f541820a4b215766451031f4eea678cee46a1b83e41.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/b8b31d46646db2d88d8a3f5658ee876383795ad5ba9aa55eb9959d5f95c02dbe.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-127032/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b7b8782-d1f4-5654-a18f-6ad35de8fb30"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127032"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-127025/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "8f9fba82-f8ec-5d68-9cf0-34bfe3f3eaf8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127025/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "420a9bd8-14bc-557f-85f6-b0e57965ad90" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127025/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "8dcd6b47-1ae7-5b5b-b300-2309525c72de" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127025/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "364c0867-f976-57d3-98cd-107221cb6356" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127025/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "47a98565-85da-5627-bee7-d38ae27b1d16" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127025/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6a012003-4702-5501-b5bd-179e85b82f93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127025/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-127025/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-127025/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-127025/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-127025/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-127025/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127025" :question/id #uuid "e08496f2-05a9-5a3b-bb4a-6a9443c2f0cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-127025" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/95e7e2df02e4694d85be868f348bb65fb6a7bf2c646b89000b9af655f937a12f.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive\n- The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement III is false. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/bfd6a4f3d368bc5920e1c79eed913f4ef21fcdf8b9eb42bbc5ce3f5c2ba7d36b.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/789b387e512c53895b0cfa499c70547046318a29da6d9af7f556a07bcac830ed.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-127025/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b7b8782-d1f4-5654-a18f-6ad35de8fb30"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127025"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-127056/selection/8b02cbbc4b7a9f571a74b0615f4d39a864bc56c66cd1e66cb1f3ba4359266083" :answer/id #uuid "95dd98e5-4cbc-52b0-96f1-9b496aae705e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127056/selection/651a4f398f4aad48657f3168baea5d24d2b2a3ba57e881643b2afd2882b738d1" :answer/id #uuid "2af9a0ec-3f73-5a84-8e49-368aa0c9b3b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x{y}^{2}+x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127056/selection/d4108b1220329461a43340b52e78139e9b6b016db72c40d1a0e3bb59a1d93e2c" :answer/id #uuid "b89efc2d-e093-5312-8ea9-3cff4dd4dcd4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{x}^{2}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127056/selection/3669bb3f4236b563450c393488e609babefaee25ded9163177dacc0157c37566" :answer/id #uuid "920945a0-a666-5128-9d87-1a685a32745b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2xy+{x}^{2}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127056/selection/2b47ddf10b716938c27d8c61f3fe420a8992d622f69cbac42f0aed7c804cd265" :answer/id #uuid "498eb49b-ee8e-5e92-aa30-302a3f483eb5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "xy+3x{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127056/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "587e0115-72da-500f-a85c-dda12f1d6d15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127056/selection/d4108b1220329461a43340b52e78139e9b6b016db72c40d1a0e3bb59a1d93e2c" :answer-field/choices ["q-127056/selection/8b02cbbc4b7a9f571a74b0615f4d39a864bc56c66cd1e66cb1f3ba4359266083" "q-127056/selection/651a4f398f4aad48657f3168baea5d24d2b2a3ba57e881643b2afd2882b738d1" "q-127056/selection/d4108b1220329461a43340b52e78139e9b6b016db72c40d1a0e3bb59a1d93e2c" "q-127056/selection/3669bb3f4236b563450c393488e609babefaee25ded9163177dacc0157c37566" "q-127056/selection/2b47ddf10b716938c27d8c61f3fe420a8992d622f69cbac42f0aed7c804cd265"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127056" :question/id #uuid "b4533f8c-3581-5325-9d06-26c4248423fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-127056" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=15xy\\,i+5{x}^{2}\\,j$ and the circle $C$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25.$ If the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $n=\\frac{x}{5}\\,i+\\frac{y}{5}\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/d3d68329d81150c4a975bae4963d4e480cefaef0f2a0a29a0648c3793e9fb462.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(15xy\\,i+5{x}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{x}{5}\\,i+\\frac{y}{5}\\,j)\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}3{x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}4{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $4{x}^{2}y.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-127056/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9c8ca56-5ea8-547c-80ab-6b1f1347447e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127056"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-127049/selection/09084426c70d82ebdf2b731a92d2a7d8bcf3e11aef7a33f1980bd97224d9889a" :answer/id #uuid "817eaed9-aac7-54b8-9d58-46629a352db4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127049/selection/fb4e6582e602490af6b54bd2532c5d38419e46bfa3a562f64277f425aa97edb9" :answer/id #uuid "1ada45af-adf9-5687-9059-47a48e42de1b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127049/selection/914de60243220336036ec357ffb8f0fa445ca66fd2d3c4ad2717d6f0971ebe5f" :answer/id #uuid "a9e4cdce-6d60-5d27-8767-09840e554cf3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127049/selection/d59853570c5b460ce82039da4c45edbb24defb7000d6534b07ba956d55440103" :answer/id #uuid "19c86fc1-b324-5592-ac46-77db91ca2ee4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127049/selection/76fb250abeb7f209ab85335c227e6534b3adb1d5dda2caa40d781a427c029949" :answer/id #uuid "22d4f0cd-20ce-55c9-b00b-7d768b392fe0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{4}{y}^{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127049/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "54d2a749-f5dc-5d22-a107-bfd03b701359" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127049/selection/914de60243220336036ec357ffb8f0fa445ca66fd2d3c4ad2717d6f0971ebe5f" :answer-field/choices ["q-127049/selection/09084426c70d82ebdf2b731a92d2a7d8bcf3e11aef7a33f1980bd97224d9889a" "q-127049/selection/fb4e6582e602490af6b54bd2532c5d38419e46bfa3a562f64277f425aa97edb9" "q-127049/selection/914de60243220336036ec357ffb8f0fa445ca66fd2d3c4ad2717d6f0971ebe5f" "q-127049/selection/d59853570c5b460ce82039da4c45edbb24defb7000d6534b07ba956d55440103" "q-127049/selection/76fb250abeb7f209ab85335c227e6534b3adb1d5dda2caa40d781a427c029949"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127049" :question/id #uuid "cc107592-3a31-505e-b22b-e74c3d756b36" :question/math-academy-id "q-127049" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=2{x}^{2}\\,i+2{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the circle $C$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ If the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $n=\\frac{x}{2}\\,i+\\frac{y}{2}\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/ff84680c55f5349431cf3d70066eeaba2c680569faa3b2210e5d14ef63ccd99e.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(2{x}^{2}\\,i+2{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{x}{2}i+\\frac{y}{2}\\,j)\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{3}+{y}^{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-127049/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9c8ca56-5ea8-547c-80ab-6b1f1347447e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127049"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-126833"] :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the circle $C$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ If the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $n=x\\,i+y\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/386f010932b5846e7f47a605a94a5b5d580bd35dbe37512b1583d15d29f3dc70.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}({x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (xi+y\\,j)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}{x}^{3}-{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{3}-{y}^{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-127057/selection/757ae8f9db34c86d8d145dce28534f21ff3af8b27385ac4c5258e45c148bf761" :answer/id #uuid "34cb5191-d220-513e-973e-4f97ae268279" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{x}^{2}y+{y}^{3}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127057/selection/310887628c68e978a23a80c717205cff509ffed7a6302bb6b7f04495b32f6377" :answer/id #uuid "07a704fa-ec5c-59db-9f0a-6a4c76554cba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x+{y}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127057/selection/bab3ae7e56621252d1d0f3af910616ad7548f525ebc6643c6920f1c1ae76e07c" :answer/id #uuid "ec4ce701-ff60-5a2e-93d2-2d5e67dd93da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6x{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127057/selection/5710d32d1d2127328c282baca754e30e98a0a0c27805e3b6b84ed2095dfd4b56" :answer/id #uuid "91883fcb-92cf-51cf-9272-6a3d73347fb9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127057/selection/5f0831c79d61988924a0b470fc65382b099357e021676a32cc881fce4dc405d2" :answer/id #uuid "aa1dfb4d-37e8-5dd7-a721-d71306ab6273" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x{y}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127057/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "27171527-c232-535a-8f48-2fe7e205416c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127057/selection/5710d32d1d2127328c282baca754e30e98a0a0c27805e3b6b84ed2095dfd4b56" :answer-field/choices ["q-127057/selection/757ae8f9db34c86d8d145dce28534f21ff3af8b27385ac4c5258e45c148bf761" "q-127057/selection/310887628c68e978a23a80c717205cff509ffed7a6302bb6b7f04495b32f6377" "q-127057/selection/bab3ae7e56621252d1d0f3af910616ad7548f525ebc6643c6920f1c1ae76e07c" "q-127057/selection/5710d32d1d2127328c282baca754e30e98a0a0c27805e3b6b84ed2095dfd4b56" "q-127057/selection/5f0831c79d61988924a0b470fc65382b099357e021676a32cc881fce4dc405d2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127057" :question/id #uuid "05a5811d-d732-5c24-84a4-d070b12f61bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-127057" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,i+2{x}^{3}y\\,j$ and the circle $C$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ If the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by  \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $n=\\frac{x}{2}\\,i+\\frac{y}{2}\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/ff84680c55f5349431cf3d70066eeaba2c680569faa3b2210e5d14ef63ccd99e.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,i+2{x}^{3}y\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{x}{2}i+\\frac{y}{2}\\,j)\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-127057/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9c8ca56-5ea8-547c-80ab-6b1f1347447e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127057"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-127050/selection/39df1e289bcd737ec28eb793e91a13b2832f80f4efc359f03b3c5e44f095b32b" :answer/id #uuid "09c40e3a-d109-5e20-a1d5-8e396d0bbd9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127050/selection/58292fbf42dc88461b8ebfe73b23770d6b7409f9baacb13f1b732cc5fa0401cc" :answer/id #uuid "ed28d01f-bc38-5d9d-9163-725ce9fe3081" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127050/selection/c8956e2d5786c4f2e53be3727ef9c087bca09fcc520828d2db129464404d2e15" :answer/id #uuid "9ef8c0fc-4e9f-58e6-92b2-16ed18a6a9da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127050/selection/d3aef67bd612f73190f7474958e335f23223e8003360392f9c3eb294420a9650" :answer/id #uuid "b2150b83-e401-5b68-b31b-fed8ec7403ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127050/selection/03fb15883c252c6b4762a35ee361b6f3d3baaded8f4e4feb63fb36795028764f" :answer/id #uuid "1215f098-5fc4-5218-bfa9-e557b501fe00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{3}{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127050/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6b421dd2-7ae8-564d-ac09-6817620f6879" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127050/selection/58292fbf42dc88461b8ebfe73b23770d6b7409f9baacb13f1b732cc5fa0401cc" :answer-field/choices ["q-127050/selection/39df1e289bcd737ec28eb793e91a13b2832f80f4efc359f03b3c5e44f095b32b" "q-127050/selection/58292fbf42dc88461b8ebfe73b23770d6b7409f9baacb13f1b732cc5fa0401cc" "q-127050/selection/c8956e2d5786c4f2e53be3727ef9c087bca09fcc520828d2db129464404d2e15" "q-127050/selection/d3aef67bd612f73190f7474958e335f23223e8003360392f9c3eb294420a9650" "q-127050/selection/03fb15883c252c6b4762a35ee361b6f3d3baaded8f4e4feb63fb36795028764f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127050" :question/id #uuid "83f77d00-0a21-52bf-87bb-6b8cfabd19ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-127050" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/4f8af75aece2346ffbf7a09e1ee90b6e0270f5661dd74e068abb2b2a848e5b97.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{3}\\,i+3{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{2}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $y$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=j.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/a83c3476bbe2ed2872ed8938dda59c5d0e6d58d05ecaf662748e7a184a4994a4.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}({x}^{3}\\,i+3{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $3{y}^{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-127050/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9c8ca56-5ea8-547c-80ab-6b1f1347447e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127050"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-126890/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "8c5907fe-14ad-525a-a78b-c6d5d1b95f4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126890/selection/0fbe558572ecce67593db00d22f0d8d9d09c98d019451cd13488790a441e6c9c" :answer/id #uuid "c68754a5-6ba1-5fb4-80a6-a823ce91c8ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126890/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" :answer/id #uuid "5bc6088c-3c8a-552e-9a6b-a790e48708b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126890/selection/0674b229b15216f6ea870ccaaf87984357f5d6af66421aad0753c15d921d419a" :answer/id #uuid "0b34799f-b5bd-5d84-8da2-db48d1f372b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "64" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126890/selection/a38ef12f135c1e6010d68ed21f81bc27372a73a756455b7066f6182c46018cc8" :answer/id #uuid "12556970-d28f-54c2-8717-976fab275521" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126890/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8970edfd-234a-5371-9d3b-9683efaf4dcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-126890/selection/a38ef12f135c1e6010d68ed21f81bc27372a73a756455b7066f6182c46018cc8" :answer-field/choices ["q-126890/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-126890/selection/0fbe558572ecce67593db00d22f0d8d9d09c98d019451cd13488790a441e6c9c" "q-126890/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" "q-126890/selection/0674b229b15216f6ea870ccaaf87984357f5d6af66421aad0753c15d921d419a" "q-126890/selection/a38ef12f135c1e6010d68ed21f81bc27372a73a756455b7066f6182c46018cc8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-126890" :question/id #uuid "81aec798-829c-5c5f-bf16-6135dcf124f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-126890" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/70a51bcda43cd04a56c6b87a10723258c995e8daae87689a2ae8af5f44112ead.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=x\\,i+y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(x\\,i+y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(2-(-2)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot 8=32.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-126890/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "349c29ae-5eb5-5454-a98f-c1adac3d7108"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-126890"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-127190/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "34f6aa75-391e-5406-8a9f-7db3a0a58c42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127190/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "bf36e8a0-b36a-5948-be4d-8c1b8f093030" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127190/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "a0d6ad4f-13fc-5f35-b7f3-6a951c7f3531" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127190/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "e544a02e-eaa4-5e72-9519-ef5a8265fbcb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127190/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "c7cc7c58-812b-530a-9e8d-cad857e63e3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127190/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8a097576-867a-513a-ba36-bdfa1aa51298" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127190/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-127190/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-127190/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-127190/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-127190/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" "q-127190/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127190" :question/id #uuid "48ac2d19-52ca-52b9-937f-2b3ce74513d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-127190" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/ea53225d28411f38006861d6c8dc46f8d3d1ea01b3bb6183954f68e7124c5682.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=3\\,i$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/1de4c4c55784b4a84a0493a4cc65082c430cadcc39c3d808001b2fd27665e968.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $F=3\\,i$ is parallel to ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{4},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net outflow through ${C}_{1}$ will cancel with the net inflow through ${C}_{3}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s\\,⟹\\,{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-127190/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "349c29ae-5eb5-5454-a98f-c1adac3d7108"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127190"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-127194/selection/f509030681214332b5989836fe0af4b2eb5858d193e689fd8441dff5cd45ff45" :answer/id #uuid "d8b461c2-022f-5193-a469-126eefc13737" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127194/selection/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" :answer/id #uuid "0a65b489-0c15-599a-9685-17768b86c134" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127194/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer/id #uuid "b6feb1b2-f945-515c-84e3-64c7f43ea8d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127194/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "6484e7bf-48a3-508d-99f9-5581476ca0ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127194/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "ce14208e-c971-568f-9798-ade8c298e034" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127194/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "07a62730-c039-570e-8b83-279b579bd24d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127194/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-127194/selection/f509030681214332b5989836fe0af4b2eb5858d193e689fd8441dff5cd45ff45" "q-127194/selection/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" "q-127194/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" "q-127194/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" "q-127194/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127194" :question/id #uuid "f94b8b25-2498-5e25-896e-f58f1febb61d" :question/math-academy-id "q-127194" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/8e1767b9bea45e742df4953f0ec50b2d43c9622994035342307dc2be6abba8de.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-2\\,i$ and a square $C$ with side length $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/878a9992405e5968a03a04d12f42aeb85bca52c07dc7081244b04011643a74bf.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $F=-2\\,i$ is parallel to ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{4},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net inflow through ${C}_{1}$ will cancel with the net outflow through ${C}_{3}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s\\,⟹\\,{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-127194/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "349c29ae-5eb5-5454-a98f-c1adac3d7108"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127194"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-126881"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/19fca0188077e56d6b56048d1cd48ea840922cafe7873aaa02937c8c2845e998.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=2\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $F=2\\,j$ is parallel to ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{3},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net outflow through ${C}_{2}$ will cancel with the net inflow through ${C}_{4}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s\\,⟹\\,{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-127198/selection/4644593a6ae896793c595e8a37cd468ba91be6a8038e715c4c88cddad1968793" :answer/id #uuid "32e2a87a-277c-59dc-ac42-880f15b9fca1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127198/selection/4e0d8402ce61a9969d16a551582a4c98e6792e5edb3c1272d991dba7d5c92279" :answer/id #uuid "feff3d47-b74d-5030-a612-835d56dacd65" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127198/selection/94b1e608a1bece13c117321c62644f2051416b0003719c2dd4b9ea44af9d6b64" :answer/id #uuid "6518b076-2b56-596f-8afe-a82e2211a888" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-28" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127198/selection/ce9ad5740269388d77893fbd186fe766ceb1482af44117dcfc7940e9dca5a239" :answer/id #uuid "e2e3310c-688d-5842-b878-1d9854e922a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127198/selection/34d6a116cfea25ad11008d41d6152ee565691a4dc671039515875488f5520d96" :answer/id #uuid "2e1d4400-8cfc-57ed-a01a-9a4ed7da2c44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127198/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "98e0217d-5290-562c-bcd4-831b556096ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127198/selection/ce9ad5740269388d77893fbd186fe766ceb1482af44117dcfc7940e9dca5a239" :answer-field/choices ["q-127198/selection/4644593a6ae896793c595e8a37cd468ba91be6a8038e715c4c88cddad1968793" "q-127198/selection/4e0d8402ce61a9969d16a551582a4c98e6792e5edb3c1272d991dba7d5c92279" "q-127198/selection/94b1e608a1bece13c117321c62644f2051416b0003719c2dd4b9ea44af9d6b64" "q-127198/selection/ce9ad5740269388d77893fbd186fe766ceb1482af44117dcfc7940e9dca5a239" "q-127198/selection/34d6a116cfea25ad11008d41d6152ee565691a4dc671039515875488f5520d96"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127198" :question/id #uuid "50bf3cd3-875c-5457-a7a0-cb2daf3cac97" :question/math-academy-id "q-127198" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/fdb05b3fcbfcfdab8344eaaaf54584a95f20696618b412873ddeb219b7a401b3.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-x\\,i-y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(-x\\,i-y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2(2-(-2)) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot (-8)=-32.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-127198/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "349c29ae-5eb5-5454-a98f-c1adac3d7108"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127198"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-226152/selection/cee37ae0a2921bd3973a5cb0592c822dbbfafcfefba8d7007910ead797b9112f" :answer/id #uuid "dd2c657a-8284-5ac0-a027-35a216842f6c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-226152/selection/f345fac9a81ccd096b9959c6e7a966a446e93e73b82d111a376f9064132509fd" :answer/id #uuid "ee49900b-a164-58a6-b1bf-958719eefb8a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-226152/selection/695792c47319cb1ed098a6d83038b7ad6d032838f9103d693bf346f1dc234ff8" :answer/id #uuid "590066e2-34f7-5a51-bf80-f7a40f119dc8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-226152/selection/713bb3b087b31554fba8b48520fba2f9d4d353e9b2d87f3d41c293daf48e1776" :answer/id #uuid "507a2a13-b87a-5138-9332-cec9774b14ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-226152/selection/a38ef12f135c1e6010d68ed21f81bc27372a73a756455b7066f6182c46018cc8" :answer/id #uuid "51e1872e-3feb-5ce6-a2aa-8a41c77ed404" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-226152/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7e6c0fc5-5b26-5950-a0d6-6c4f085b20d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-226152/selection/f345fac9a81ccd096b9959c6e7a966a446e93e73b82d111a376f9064132509fd" :answer-field/choices ["q-226152/selection/cee37ae0a2921bd3973a5cb0592c822dbbfafcfefba8d7007910ead797b9112f" "q-226152/selection/f345fac9a81ccd096b9959c6e7a966a446e93e73b82d111a376f9064132509fd" "q-226152/selection/695792c47319cb1ed098a6d83038b7ad6d032838f9103d693bf346f1dc234ff8" "q-226152/selection/713bb3b087b31554fba8b48520fba2f9d4d353e9b2d87f3d41c293daf48e1776" "q-226152/selection/a38ef12f135c1e6010d68ed21f81bc27372a73a756455b7066f6182c46018cc8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-226152" :question/id #uuid "4505faab-db54-5c25-8832-44e81540c7f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-226152" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/0ac0fa01b3e09d268115a604a53ca6247c9e26d88603482223aa43927df20561.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-2x\\,i+5y\\,j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $2$ and height $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/29f4b57751e27f01539fb48acf115b6e255d47a04ee647ef85aa496731b266c8.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(-2x\\,i+5y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=-8.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(-2x\\,i+5y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+2\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =5{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=20.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot (-8)+2\\cdot 20=24.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-226152/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "446c5e28-00d3-5a17-ad90-0e66b5ec3a59"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-226152"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-127207/selection/4644593a6ae896793c595e8a37cd468ba91be6a8038e715c4c88cddad1968793" :answer/id #uuid "bfc6b192-4e76-5261-8305-742fa21ab2e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127207/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" :answer/id #uuid "30165b1c-b425-527f-afc7-a2c1056a659f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127207/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "24b35cc1-d756-5546-b9f4-3fc978fd5b67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127207/selection/34d6a116cfea25ad11008d41d6152ee565691a4dc671039515875488f5520d96" :answer/id #uuid "6874c52d-a3e8-5c4d-ae3d-50b6b8d9579d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127207/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "b4ba691b-3b17-5142-a2af-fb755b666e9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127207/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "68f37b52-f2a5-53b2-ad19-073a77192b5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127207/selection/34d6a116cfea25ad11008d41d6152ee565691a4dc671039515875488f5520d96" :answer-field/choices ["q-127207/selection/4644593a6ae896793c595e8a37cd468ba91be6a8038e715c4c88cddad1968793" "q-127207/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" "q-127207/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" "q-127207/selection/34d6a116cfea25ad11008d41d6152ee565691a4dc671039515875488f5520d96" "q-127207/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127207" :question/id #uuid "3324984f-18d1-5a09-86e5-6e3d00d1319b" :question/math-academy-id "q-127207" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/6696cf798bb4ecdf8c6eaeb65b0bb49477b04207c22bceff1e2cdee8614b1728.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=x\\,i-2y\\,j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $2$ and height $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/aba4a2e6603e65d3f67e65c5af73b2cdda6bdf798d9e9d09005f738d700500ea.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=4.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+2\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=-8.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 4+2\\cdot (-8)=-8.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-127207/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "446c5e28-00d3-5a17-ad90-0e66b5ec3a59"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127207"] :db/ensure 